lows Num semantics)', 'description': "Validate that the max operation respects the Num implementation's handling of special values such as NaN."}]}, {'group': 'NumericIndicator.max(Indicator<Num>) - same instance', 'cases': [{'input': [{'parameter': 'other', 'value': 'the same NumericIndicator instance (this)'}], 'expected': 'NumericIndicator whose getValue(0)=10, getValue(1)=-20, getValue(2)=30 (identical to the original indicator)', 'description': 'Calling max with the same instance should return an indicator that yields exactly the original values for every index.'}]}, {'group': 'NumericIndicator.max(Indicator<Num>) - commutativity', 'cases': [{'input': [{'parameter': 'other', 'value': 'ConstantIndicator of Num values [10, 10, 30]'}], 'expected': 'Two NumericIndicators (a.max(b) and b.max(a)) both return values [10, 15, 30] for indices 0‑2, proving the operation is commutative.', 'description': 'Validate that max(a, b) yields the same result as max(b, a) for a=[5,15,25] and b=[10,10,30].'}]}, {'group': 'NumericIndicator.max(Indicator<Num>) - NaN in this indicator', 'cases': [{'input': [{'parameter': 'other', 'value': 'ConstantIndicator of Num values [1, 2, 4]'}], 'expected': 'NumericIndicator whose getValue(0)=NaN, getValue(1)=2, getValue(2)=4 (NaN propagates, other values selected when larger)', 'description': 'When the left‑hand indicator contains NaN at an index, the max operation should propagate NaN according to Num semantics.'}]}, {'group': 'NumericIndicator.max(Indicator<Num>) - infinities', 'cases': [{'input': [{'parameter': 'other', 'value': 'ConstantIndicator of Num values [100, POSITIVE_INFINITY, NEGATIVE_INFINITY]'}], 'expected': 'NumericIndicator whose getValue(0)=POSITIVE_INFINITY, getValue(1)=POSITIVE_INFINITY, getValue(2)=-5 (the larger of -5 and NEGATIVE_INFINITY)', 'description': 'Test handling of POSITIVE_INFINITY and NEGATIVE_INFINITY values on both sides of the max operation.'}]}, {'group': 'NumericIndicator.max(Indicator<Num>) - negative numbers', 'cases': [{'input': [{'parameter': 'other', 'value': 'ConstantIndicator of Num values [-20, -3, -1]'}], 'expected': 'NumericIndicator whose getValue(0)=-10, getValue(1)=-3, getValue(2)=0 (the greater (less negative) value per index)', 'description': 'Both indicators contain only negative numbers; max should return the numerically larger (i.e., closer to zero) value for each index.'}]}, {'group': 'NumericIndicator.max(Indicator<Num>) - mixed NaN and infinities', 'cases': [{'input': [{'parameter': 'other', 'value': 'ConstantIndicator of Num values [POSITIVE_INFINITY, 0, NaN]'}], 'expected': 'NumericIndicator whose getValue(0)=NaN, getValue(1)=0, getValue(2)=NaN (NaN propagates, POSITIVE_INFINITY wins over NEGATIVE_INFINITY, 0 wins over NEGATIVE_INFINITY)', 'description': "Combine special values (NaN, POSITIVE_INFINITY, NEGATIVE_INFINITY) on both sides to verify the max operation follows Num's defined ordering."}]}, {'group': 'NumericIndicator.max(Indicator<Num>) - derived indicator (SMA)', 'cases': [{'input': [{'parameter': 'other', 'value': 'SMAIndicator built on the same series with barCount=2'}], 'expected': 'NumericIndicator whose getValue(0)=15, getValue(1)=25, getValue(2)=35 (max between constant [10,20,30] and SMA [15,25,35])', 'description': 'Use a non‑constant derived indicator (Simple Moving Average) to ensure max works correctly with lazy‑evaluated indicators.'}]}]
